{
  "opening": "This release marks a significant milestone for Arrow, introducing Python 3.9 support and dropping legacy versions while refining core behaviors. We are also enhancing precision in time span calculations and improving error handling across the library. Please ensure all public API changes described below are implemented to maintain consistency and usability.",
  "requirements": [
    {
      "id": "m03",
      "text": "The DateTimeParser must raise a ParserError instead of a TypeError or regular error when string parsing fails to match specific patterns."
    },
    {
      "id": "m05",
      "text": "The JapaneseLocale must provide corrected translations for human-readable date and time strings to fix previous inaccuracies."
    },
    {
      "id": "m06",
      "text": "The Arrow.humanize method with granularity set to 'auto' must apply more accurate limits to prevent unusual or imprecise human-readable output."
    },
    {
      "id": "m07",
      "text": "The library must officially drop support for Python 2.7 and 3.5, ensuring that any remaining references to these versions are removed from the public API and documentation."
    },
    {
      "id": "m09",
      "text": "Handling of maximum permitted timestamps on Windows systems must be corrected to avoid errors, and utility functions like total_seconds should be removed or refactored internally without changing external behavior."
    },
    {
      "id": "m11",
      "text": "The span, span_range, and interval methods must accept an 'exact' keyword argument that ensures timespans begin at the start time and do not extend beyond the end time provided."
    },
    {
      "id": "m12",
      "text": "The Arrow class must support a new fromordinal method and the isoformat method must accept a timespec argument to control ISO 8601 string precision."
    },
    {
      "id": "m19",
      "text": "Passing None to the ArrowFactory.get method must raise a TypeError with a descriptive message instead of allowing invalid input."
    }
  ],
  "closing": "These changes collectively improve the robustness, accuracy, and modern compatibility of the library. Thank you for your attention to these details in the upcoming release."
}
